# Banjo-Tooie (US) seeds of jjjj12212's banjo_tooie world. Unlike every other profile here,
# none of these addresses are in the game's own code -- 99.6% of Banjo-Tooie's ROM is
# compressed and the game is decompressed into RAM at boot, so there is no instruction in
# the ROM to retarget and no dead game code to host a stub. Measured: the retail image is
# 7.97 bits/byte from 0x14000 on, and `core1` at the offset the decomp names disassembles
# to garbage. Only the boot segment (rom 0x1000..0x50E0) and the header are plain.
#
# What IS uncompressed is the 143,452 bytes the randomizer appends at rom 0x2000000. Its
# patched boot segment DMAs that to 0x80400000 (PI_WR_LEN 0x00100000, so 0x100001 bytes)
# and jumps to 0x80400004. So for the appended region vram = rom + 0x7E400000, and both the
# hook and the stub live there -- which means these pins are on the randomizer's code and
# move with every apworld release, not just with the game. See issue #280.
#
# The ROM is seed-independent: the client applies one static bsdiff and asserts a fixed
# output md5 (1876534db1ec87691faf474f461619d8), so a pin failing means the apworld version
# changed rather than that this seed is unusual.

# 4 KB of zeros inside the BTHACK's own padding, at rom 0x201CD64. It is inside the boot
# DMA's range, so the randomizer's own loader brings the stub into RAM -- nothing else has
# to. watch-ranges saw all eight 512-byte pieces stay zero across 14,160 frames while the
# neighbouring slice changed 227 times, which is the control that makes "clean" mean clean.
# The wider 26 KB run of ROM zeros around it is NOT dead: the BTHACK uses it as working
# memory, and four of its slices already held non-zero bytes at the first sample.
STUB_VRAM=0x8041CD64
STUB_MAX=4096

# Measured with the whole Archipelago stack live over ~51 minutes: 990 of 1024 base pages
# used, and 35 of 1024 Expansion Pak pages, unchanged from the first sample to the last.
# Banjo-Tooie is the first candidate here that REQUIRES an Expansion Pak for itself, so the
# Pak being free could not be assumed -- it requires its presence and uses 140 KB of it.
#
# INSIDE the randomizer's 1 MB boot DMA (0x80400000..0x80500001), deliberately. The first
# design put the agent above that window and had the stub copy it with the game's own ROM
# routine; it black-screened a console, and a bisect on hardware showed the copy was the
# only step that did. That routine runs once, at boot, and a raw PI transfer started from
# a frame hook does not survive the game's own traffic.
#
# So the agent rides the boot DMA in: rom 0x2080000 maps to 0x80480000, it is in RAM
# before the first frame, and the stub never copies anything. profiles/oot works the same
# way, leaning on the game's own loader.
#
# 0x80480000 is 350 KB clear of the BTHACK's real content, which ends at 0x8042305C, and
# the agent's 25,888 B ends at 0x80486520, still inside the DMA's window. The page scanner
# reported this whole range untouched, which it would have said either way -- the DMA fills
# it from past the ROM's end and that lands as zeros -- so the clearance above comes from
# the BTHACK's measured size, not from the scan.
AGENT_VRAM=0x80480000

# vram - 0x7E400000, so the boot DMA carries it. Must stay inside rom 0x2000000..0x2100001
# or nothing loads the agent at all, which is why profile.toml pins that DMA's instructions.
AGENT_ROM=0x2080000

# The retail game requires the Expansion Pak, so a seed cannot run without one anyway.
AGENT_MIN_RAM=0x800000
